/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES German batch 23: native roots for the
   map-adventure words that had none. id | 中文 | English.
   ===================================================== */
(function () {
  const RAW = String.raw`
d_sohn|Sohn(儿子)来自日耳曼语 *sunuz,印欧语 *suHnús(所生者),出自 *sewH-(生)——与梵语 sūnú、立陶宛语 sūnùs、英语 son 同根;希腊语 hyios(儿子)可能也是。拉丁语用的是另一词 filius("吃奶的")。Sohnemann(小子)、Schwiegersohn(女婿)、der verlorene Sohn(浪子)、Menschensohn(人子)同族。|Sohn (a son) comes from Germanic *sunuz, PIE *suHnús (the one born), from *sewH- (to give birth) — cognate with Sanskrit sūnú, Lithuanian sūnùs and English son; Greek hyios is probably kin. Latin used a different word, filius (the suckling). Sohnemann (sonny), Schwiegersohn (son-in-law), der verlorene Sohn (the prodigal son) and Menschensohn (the Son of Man) belong here.
d_tochter|Tochter(女儿)来自日耳曼语 *duhtēr,印欧语 *dʰugh₂tḗr——与希腊语 thygatēr、梵语 duhitṛ́、英语 daughter、立陶宛语 duktė̃ 同根。旧说把它解释为"挤奶的人"(*dʰewgʰ- 挤奶),很流行,但现代学者多认为不可靠——照实说:原义不明。拉丁语的 filia 是另一个词。Tochtergesellschaft(子公司——德语用"女儿公司"!)、Stieftochter 同族。|Tochter (a daughter) comes from Germanic *duhtēr, PIE *dʰugh₂tḗr — cognate with Greek thygatēr, Sanskrit duhitṛ́, English daughter and Lithuanian duktė̃. The old explanation, the milkmaid (from *dʰewgʰ-, to milk), is popular but most modern scholars reject it: the original sense is unknown. Latin filia is a different word. Tochtergesellschaft (a subsidiary — German calls it a daughter company!) and Stieftochter (stepdaughter) belong here.
d_magd|Mädchen(女孩)是 Magd(少女、女仆)的小称 Mägdchen 缩成的——日耳曼语 *magaþs(少女),与哥特语 magaþs、英语 maid/maiden 同根,可能出自 *maguz(男孩)。Magd 后来降为"女仆",于是小称 Mädchen 接替"女孩"义;因为 -chen 小称词一律中性,所以是 das Mädchen——语法性别跟后缀走,不跟人走!Jungfrau(处女)的旧说法 Maid 同族。|Mädchen (a girl) is a contraction of Mägdchen, the diminutive of Magd (a maiden, a maid) — Germanic *magaþs, cognate with Gothic magaþs and English maid, maiden, perhaps from *maguz (a boy). Magd sank to mean a servant girl, so the diminutive took over the sense girl; and because every -chen diminutive is neuter, it is das Mädchen — grammatical gender follows the suffix, not the person!
d_well|Welle(波浪)来自古高地德语 wella,是 wallen(翻滚、沸腾)的名词,印欧语 *wel-(转、滚)——与拉丁语 volvere(滚)、英语 well(泉涌、井)、walk 同根:波浪是"翻滚的水"。物理学的 Welle(波:Lichtwelle 光波、Kurzwelle 短波、Wellenlänge 波长)、Hitzewelle(热浪)、La-Ola-Welle(人浪)同族。|Welle (a wave) comes from Old High German wella, the noun of wallen (to surge, to boil), PIE *wel- (to turn, to roll) — kin to Latin volvere, English well (to well up) and walk: a wave is rolling water. Physics' Welle (Lichtwelle a light wave, Kurzwelle short wave, Wellenlänge wavelength), Hitzewelle (a heatwave) and the La-Ola-Welle (a Mexican wave) belong here.
d_sturm|Sturm(风暴)来自日耳曼语 *sturmaz(古英语 storm、古挪威语 stormr),印欧语 *(s)twer-(搅动)——与拉丁语 turba(骚乱,见 turb)同根:风暴是"搅动"。Sturm und Drang(狂飙突进,1770 年代德国文学运动,得名于克林格 1776 年的剧本)、stürmen(冲锋,Sturmangriff 攻击)、Stürmer(足球前锋!)、Wasserglas-Sturm(杯中风暴)同族。|Sturm (a storm) comes from Germanic *sturmaz (Old English storm, Old Norse stormr), PIE *(s)twer- (to stir) — kin to Latin turba (turmoil — see turb): a storm is a stirring. Sturm und Drang (the literary movement of the 1770s, named after Klinger's 1776 play), stürmen (to charge — Sturmangriff an assault), Stürmer (a football striker!) and Sturm im Wasserglas (a storm in a teacup) belong here.
d_hafen|Hafen(港口)是低地德语 haven(避风处)进入高地德语的——日耳曼语 *habnō,与英语 haven(避难所)、古挪威语 hǫfn 同根,可能出自 *habjaną(拿住、容纳——heben/haben)。港口就是"把船容纳住的地方"。Hamburger Hafen、Flughafen(机场——"飞行的港"!)、Hafenstadt、Kopenhagen(København"商人之港")同族。|Hafen (a harbour) came into High German from Low German haven (a shelter) — Germanic *habnō, cognate with English haven and Old Norse hǫfn, probably from *habjaną (to hold, to contain — heben, haben): a harbour holds the ships. Hamburger Hafen, Flughafen (an airport — a flying harbour!), Hafenstadt (a port city) and Copenhagen (København, the merchants' harbour) belong here.
d_wolk|Wolke(云)来自古高地德语 wolkan,日耳曼语 *wulkną(潮湿的东西),印欧语 *welg-(湿)——与古英语 wolcen(云;welkin 天空)、立陶宛语 vilgyti(弄湿)同根:云是"湿气"。Wolkenkratzer(摩天楼——"刮云的",skyscraper 的翻译)、Wolkenbruch(暴雨——"云破了")、aus allen Wolken fallen(大吃一惊——"从云端跌下")同族。|Wolke (a cloud) comes from Old High German wolkan, Germanic *wulkną (the damp thing), PIE *welg- (wet) — cognate with Old English wolcen (cloud; welkin, the sky) and Lithuanian vilgyti (to moisten): a cloud is moisture. Wolkenkratzer (a skyscraper — the cloud-scraper), Wolkenbruch (a cloudburst) and aus allen Wolken fallen (to be thunderstruck — to fall from all the clouds) belong here.
d_halle|Halle(大厅)来自日耳曼语 *hallō(有顶的场所),印欧语 *ḱel-(遮盖、藏)——与 hehlen(藏)、Hülle(外壳)、Hölle(地狱)、拉丁语 celare(藏)、cella(小室——Zelle!)同根:大厅是"遮盖住的地方"。英语 hall、Walhalla(瓦尔哈拉——阵亡者之厅)同源。Turnhalle(体育馆)、Bahnhofshalle(车站大厅)、Messehalle(展馆)、Markthalle 同族。注意:与 hallen(回响)无关。|Halle (a hall) comes from Germanic *hallō (a covered place), PIE *ḱel- (to cover, to hide) — kin to hehlen (to conceal), Hülle (a cover), Hölle (hell), Latin celare and cella (a small room — Zelle!): a hall is a covered place. English hall and Valhalla (the hall of the slain) are cognate. Turnhalle (a gym), Bahnhofshalle (a station concourse), Messehalle (an exhibition hall) and Markthalle belong here. Unrelated to hallen, to echo.
d_stuhl|Stuhl(椅子)来自日耳曼语 *stōlaz(座位),印欧语 *steh₂-(站、立)——"立着的东西",与 stehen、Stall、英语 stool(凳)同根。Stuhl 在德语里有很多制度义:der Heilige Stuhl(圣座——梵蒂冈)、Lehrstuhl(教席)、Stuhlgang(大便——"去椅子"!)、Rollstuhl(轮椅)、elektrischer Stuhl。|Stuhl (a chair) comes from Germanic *stōlaz (a seat), PIE *steh₂- (to stand) — the standing thing, kin to stehen, Stall and English stool. In German it carries institutional senses: der Heilige Stuhl (the Holy See), Lehrstuhl (a professorial chair), Stuhlgang (a bowel movement — going to the stool!), Rollstuhl (a wheelchair), elektrischer Stuhl.
d_faden|Faden(线)来自日耳曼语 *faþmaz(张开的双臂),印欧语 *peth₂-(张开)——原义是"双臂伸开的长度"(英语 fathom 测深单位"一英寻"=六英尺,正是它!),量线时伸开双臂,于是"一臂长"变成"线"。den Faden verlieren(忘了说到哪)、roter Faden(主线——来自歌德《亲和力》:英国海军缆绳里织入一根红线!)、Fadennudeln 同族。|Faden (a thread) comes from Germanic *faþmaz (outstretched arms), PIE *peth₂- (to spread): the length of the outspread arms (English fathom — six feet — is exactly this word!). Thread was measured by stretching the arms, so the arm-span became the thread. Den Faden verlieren (to lose the thread), roter Faden (the common thread — from Goethe's Elective Affinities, the red thread woven into Royal Navy rope!) and Fadennudeln (vermicelli) belong here.
d_schenk|Geschenk(礼物)来自 schenken(赠送),本义"斟酒、倒给人喝"——日耳曼语 *skankjaną(倒酒),与英语 skink(古语"斟")同根。给客人斟酒就是款待,所以"斟"变成"赠"。Schenke(小酒馆——"斟酒的地方")、Mundschenk(司酒官)、einschenken(斟满)、ausschenken、verschenken 同族。|Geschenk (a gift) comes from schenken (to give), which first meant to pour a drink for someone — Germanic *skankjaną (to pour out), cognate with archaic English skink. Pouring for a guest is hospitality, so pouring became giving. Schenke (a tavern — the pouring place), Mundschenk (a cupbearer), einschenken (to pour), ausschenken and verschenken belong here.
d_ring|Ring(环、戒指)来自日耳曼语 *hringaz(圈),印欧语 *(s)kreng-(弯、转)——与英语 ring、ranch?(经西班牙语 rancho,存疑)、rank(行列)同根。瓦格纳的《尼伯龙根的指环》Der Ring des Nibelungen(1876)。Ehering(婚戒)、Boxring(拳台)、Ringstraße(维也纳环城大道)、ringen(摔跤——"扭成一团")、Ringkampf 同族。|Ring (a ring) comes from Germanic *hringaz (a circle), PIE *(s)kreng- (to bend, to turn) — cognate with English ring and rank. Wagner's Der Ring des Nibelungen (1876). Ehering (a wedding ring), Boxring, Vienna's Ringstraße, ringen (to wrestle — to grapple in a knot) and Ringkampf belong here.
d_ohr|Ohr(耳)来自日耳曼语 *auzǭ,印欧语 *h₂ṓws-(耳)——与拉丁语 auris(见 auris 条)、希腊语 ous(ot-:Otologie 耳科)、英语 ear 同根。日耳曼语的 -r- 来自 -s- 的规则音变(维尔纳定律)。Ohrring(耳环)、Ohrwurm(魔性旋律——"耳中虫",earworm 就是它的翻译!)、Ohrfeige(耳光)、ganz Ohr sein(洗耳恭听)同族。|Ohr (an ear) comes from Germanic *auzǭ, PIE *h₂ṓws- — cognate with Latin auris (see auris), Greek ous (ot-: otology) and English ear. The Germanic -r- is a regular change from -s- (Verner's law). Ohrring (an earring), Ohrwurm (a tune you can't shake — English earworm is a calque of it!), Ohrfeige (a slap) and ganz Ohr sein (to be all ears) belong here.
d_nas|Nase(鼻)来自日耳曼语 *nasō,印欧语 *neh₂s-(鼻)——与拉丁语 nasus、梵语 nās、英语 nose、nostril(nose-thirl"鼻孔")同根。die Nase voll haben(受够了)、jemanden an der Nase herumführen(牵着鼻子走)、Naseweis(自作聪明的人——"鼻子灵")、Nashorn(犀牛——"鼻角"!)同族。|Nase (a nose) comes from Germanic *nasō, PIE *neh₂s- — cognate with Latin nasus, Sanskrit nās, English nose and nostril (nose-thirl, a nose-hole). Die Nase voll haben (to be fed up), jemanden an der Nase herumführen (to lead someone by the nose), Naseweis (a know-all — a keen nose) and Nashorn (a rhinoceros — nose-horn!) belong here.
d_finger|Finger(手指)来自日耳曼语 *fingraz,最可信的解释是与 fünf(五)同根(印欧语 *penkʷ-)——"五个一组的"。英语 finger、哥特语 figgrs 同源。Fingerhut(顶针——"手指帽";也是毛地黄 Digitalis!)、Fingerabdruck(指纹)、Fingerspitzengefühl(分寸感——"指尖感觉",一个难翻译的德语词)同族。|Finger (a finger) comes from Germanic *fingraz, most plausibly from the root of fünf, five (PIE *penkʷ-) — one of a set of five. English finger and Gothic figgrs are cognate. Fingerhut (a thimble — a finger hat; also the foxglove, Digitalis!), Fingerabdruck (a fingerprint) and Fingerspitzengefühl (tact — fingertip feeling, a famously untranslatable word) belong here.
d_knie|Knie(膝)来自日耳曼语 *knewą,印欧语 *ǵónu——与拉丁语 genu、希腊语 gony、梵语 jānu、英语 knee 同根(英语 k 至今不发音,但拼写保留了它)。knien(跪)、Kniefall(下跪——1970 年勃兰特在华沙犹太区起义纪念碑前的 Kniefall von Warschau!)、Kniescheibe(膝盖骨)同族。|Knie (a knee) comes from Germanic *knewą, PIE *ǵónu — cognate with Latin genu, Greek gony, Sanskrit jānu and English knee (whose k is still spelt though silent). Knien (to kneel), Kniefall (a genuflection — Willy Brandt's Kniefall von Warschau before the Ghetto Uprising memorial in 1970!) and Kniescheibe (a kneecap) belong here.
d_haar|Haar(头发)来自日耳曼语 *hēra-,印欧语 *ḱers-(竖起、粗硬)存疑——与英语 hair、古挪威语 hár 同根。haarscharf(差之毫厘——"细如发")、Haarspalterei(吹毛求疵——"劈头发")、Haare auf den Zähnen haben(不好惹)、um ein Haar(差一点)同族。|Haar (hair) comes from Germanic *hēra-, perhaps PIE *ḱers- (to bristle) — cognate with English hair and Old Norse hár. Haarscharf (by a hair's breadth), Haarspalterei (hair-splitting), Haare auf den Zähnen haben (to be formidable) and um ein Haar (within a hair) belong here.
d_zung|Zunge(舌、语言)来自日耳曼语 *tungǭ,印欧语 *dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s(舌)——与拉丁语 lingua(古拉丁 dingua!——d 变 l)、英语 tongue 同根:拉丁的 lingua 和德语 Zunge 原来是同一个词。舌头也指语言:mit fremden Zungen reden(说方言——圣灵降临的"说别国的话")、Zungenbrecher(绕口令——"折断舌头的")、eine spitze Zunge(尖刻)同族。|Zunge (a tongue, a language) comes from Germanic *tungǭ, PIE *dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s — cognate with Latin lingua (Old Latin dingua, with d becoming l!) and English tongue: Latin lingua and German Zunge were once the same word. The tongue also means language: mit fremden Zungen reden (to speak in tongues), Zungenbrecher (a tongue-twister — a tongue-breaker) and eine spitze Zunge (a sharp tongue) belong here.
d_wolf|Wolf(狼)来自日耳曼语 *wulfaz,印欧语 *wĺ̥kʷos——与拉丁语 lupus(可能是萨宾方言借词)、希腊语 lykos(Lykanthropie 狼人症)、梵语 vṛka、英语 wolf 同根。日耳曼人名里到处是狼:Wolfgang("狼行")、Adolf("高贵的狼")、Rudolf("荣耀之狼")。Wolfshunger(饿狼般的饥饿)、Wolf im Schafspelz(披羊皮的狼)同族。|Wolf comes from Germanic *wulfaz, PIE *wĺ̥kʷos — cognate with Latin lupus (probably a Sabine loan), Greek lykos (lycanthropy), Sanskrit vṛka and English wolf. Germanic names teem with wolves: Wolfgang (wolf's path), Adolf (noble wolf), Rudolf (famed wolf). Wolfshunger (a wolfish hunger) and Wolf im Schafspelz (a wolf in sheep's clothing) belong here.
d_schlang|Schlange(蛇)来自 schlingen(缠绕、盘绕)——"会缠的东西":日耳曼语 *slingwaną(甩、缠),与英语 sling(投石索)、slink 同根。Schlange stehen(排长队——像一条蛇!)、Brillenschlange(眼镜蛇;也戏称戴眼镜的人)、Schlangenlinie(蛇行线)、verschlingen(吞下)同族。|Schlange (a snake) comes from schlingen (to wind, to coil) — the thing that winds: Germanic *slingwaną (to fling, to twist), cognate with English sling and slink. Schlange stehen (to queue — like a snake!), Brillenschlange (a cobra, and jokingly a person in glasses), Schlangenlinie (a wavy line) and verschlingen (to devour) belong here.
d_adler|Adler(鹰)是中古高地德语 adel-ar("高贵的鹰")——Adel(贵族)+ Aar(鹰,古词,日耳曼语 *arô,与希腊语 ornis 鸟同根)。鹰在神圣罗马帝国与现代德国的国徽上(Bundesadler 联邦之鹰,绰号 fette Henne"肥母鸡")。Aar 本身在诗歌里还用。|Adler (an eagle) is Middle High German adel-ar, the noble eagle — Adel (nobility) + Aar (an old word for eagle, Germanic *arô, cognate with Greek ornis, a bird). The eagle is on the arms of the Holy Roman Empire and of modern Germany (the Bundesadler, nicknamed fette Henne, the fat hen). Aar survives in poetry.
d_bär|Bär(熊)来自日耳曼语 *berô,最流行的解释是"棕色的"(*bʰer-,与 braun 同根)——是一个避讳词:北方猎人不敢直呼熊的真名(印欧语 *h₂ŕ̥tḱos,见拉丁 ursus),改叫"棕家伙"。英语 bear 同源。Berlin 的城徽是熊(民间词源!实际来自斯拉夫语 berl-"沼泽")、Bärenhunger、Bärendienst(帮倒忙——"熊的服务")、jemandem einen Bären aufbinden(糊弄人)同族。|Bär (a bear) comes from Germanic *berô, most popularly explained as the brown one (*bʰer-, kin to braun) — a taboo word: northern hunters dared not speak the bear's real name (PIE *h₂ŕ̥tḱos — see Latin ursus) and called it the brown one instead. English bear is cognate. Berlin's heraldic bear is folk etymology (the name is Slavic berl-, a marsh). Bärendienst (a disservice — a bear's favour) and jemandem einen Bären aufbinden (to spin someone a yarn) belong here.
d_hirsch|Hirsch(鹿)来自日耳曼语 *herutaz(有角的),印欧语 *ḱer-(角)——与拉丁语 cervus(见 cervus)、cornu、英语 hart(雄鹿)、horn 同根。Hirschgeweih(鹿角)、Platzhirsch("领地雄鹿"——比喻:地盘老大)、Hirschkäfer(锹甲)同族。|Hirsch (a stag) comes from Germanic *herutaz (the horned one), PIE *ḱer- (horn) — cognate with Latin cervus (see cervus), cornu, English hart and horn. Hirschgeweih (antlers), Platzhirsch (the dominant stag — figuratively the top dog) and Hirschkäfer (a stag beetle) belong here.
d_kuh|Kuh(母牛)来自日耳曼语 *kōz,印欧语 *gʷṓws(牛)——与拉丁语 bos(bovis:bovine、beef 经法语)、希腊语 bous(Bosporus 牛渡!)、梵语 gáu、英语 cow 同根:同一个印欧词在拉丁里变成 b-,在日耳曼里变成 k-。Kuhhandel(讨价还价的交易)、Kuhmilch、die Kuh vom Eis holen(解决难题——"把牛从冰上牵下来")同族。|Kuh (a cow) comes from Germanic *kōz, PIE *gʷṓws — cognate with Latin bos (bovine; beef through French), Greek bous (the Bosporus is the cow's ford!), Sanskrit gáu and English cow: one Indo-European word became b- in Latin and k- in Germanic. Kuhhandel (horse-trading), Kuhmilch and die Kuh vom Eis holen (to solve a tricky problem — to get the cow off the ice) belong here.
d_schaf|Schaf(羊)来自西日耳曼语 *skēpą,来源不明——它取代了印欧语的 *h₂ówis(见拉丁 ovis、英语 ewe)。英语 sheep 同源。Schäfer(牧羊人,Schäferhund 德国牧羊犬)、schwarzes Schaf(害群之马)、Schafskopf(蠢货;也是一种巴伐利亚纸牌)、sein Schäfchen ins Trockene bringen 同族。|Schaf (a sheep) comes from West Germanic *skēpą, of unknown origin — it replaced the Indo-European *h₂ówis (see Latin ovis, English ewe). English sheep is cognate. Schäfer (a shepherd — Schäferhund, the German shepherd dog), schwarzes Schaf (the black sheep), Schafskopf (a fool, and a Bavarian card game) belong here.
d_zieg|Ziege(山羊)来自中古高地德语 zige,来源不明,可能是模仿叫唤的小儿语——古词 Geiß(<*gaits,与拉丁 haedus 小山羊、英语 goat 同根)在南德、奥地利至今更常用:地理上的词汇分界。Ziegenbock(公山羊)、Ziegenpeter(腮腺炎!)、Zicklein(小羊羔)同族。|Ziege (a goat) comes from Middle High German zige, of unknown origin, perhaps a nursery word; the older Geiß (from *gaits, cognate with Latin haedus, a kid, and English goat) is still the usual word in southern Germany and Austria — a regional vocabulary border. Ziegenbock (a billy goat), Ziegenpeter (mumps!) and Zicklein (a kid) belong here.
d_schwein|Schwein(猪)来自日耳曼语 *swīną(猪的——形容词"属于 sū 的"),出自印欧语 *suH-(猪)——与拉丁语 sus(猪)、希腊语 hys、英语 sow(母猪)、swine 同根。德语的 Schwein 还是"运气":Schwein haben(走运——可能来自射击比赛给末名发的安慰奖小猪!)、Glücksschwein(招财猪)、Sparschwein(储钱罐)、Schweinehund(innerer Schweinehund 惰性——"内心的猪狗")同族。|Schwein (a pig) comes from Germanic *swīną (belonging to the sow), from PIE *suH- (a pig) — cognate with Latin sus, Greek hys, English sow and swine. In German a pig also means luck: Schwein haben (to be lucky — perhaps from the piglet given as a booby prize at shooting contests!), Glücksschwein (a lucky pig), Sparschwein (a piggy bank) and der innere Schweinehund (one's inner laziness) belong here.
d_maus|Maus(老鼠)来自日耳曼语 *mūs,印欧语 *muh₂s——与拉丁语 mus、希腊语 mys、梵语 mūṣ、英语 mouse 同根;这是印欧语系最稳定的动物名之一。拉丁的 musculus(小老鼠)变成 muscle(肌肉——肌肉在皮下动像小老鼠!)。Mausefalle(捕鼠器)、mausetot(死透了)、Maus(电脑鼠标)同族。|Maus (a mouse) comes from Germanic *mūs, PIE *muh₂s — cognate with Latin mus, Greek mys, Sanskrit mūṣ and English mouse, one of the most stable animal names in Indo-European. Latin musculus (a little mouse) became muscle — a muscle moving under the skin looks like a mouse! Mausefalle (a mousetrap), mausetot (stone dead) and Maus (a computer mouse) belong here.
d_frosch|Frosch(青蛙)来自日耳曼语 *fruskaz(跳的东西?),印欧语 *prew-(跳)存疑——与英语 frog、古挪威语 froskr 同根。Froschkönig(青蛙王子——格林童话第一篇!)、Froschperspektive(仰视角度——"青蛙视角")、sei kein Frosch(别扭扭捏捏)、Frosch im Hals(嗓子发哑)同族。|Frosch (a frog) comes from Germanic *fruskaz (the hopper?), perhaps PIE *prew- (to jump) — cognate with English frog and Old Norse froskr. Der Froschkönig (the Frog Prince — the first of Grimms' tales!), Froschperspektive (a worm's-eye view — the frog's perspective), sei kein Frosch (don't be a spoilsport) and ein Frosch im Hals (a frog in the throat) belong here.
d_bien|Biene(蜜蜂)来自日耳曼语 *bijō,印欧语 *bʰey-(蜂)——与英语 bee、古爱尔兰语 bech 同根。拉丁的 apis 是另一个词。Bienenstock(蜂箱)、Bienenfleiß(勤劳——"蜜蜂般的勤奋")、fleißiges Bienchen、Bienensterben(蜂群消失)同族。|Biene (a bee) comes from Germanic *bijō, PIE *bʰey- — cognate with English bee and Old Irish bech. Latin apis is a different word. Bienenstock (a beehive), Bienenfleiß (bee-like diligence), fleißiges Bienchen and Bienensterben (colony collapse) belong here.
d_vier|vier(四)来自日耳曼语 *fedwōr,印欧语 *kʷetwóres——与拉丁语 quattuor(quadr-、quart-)、希腊语 tettares(tetra-)、梵语 catvā́ras、英语 four 同根。日耳曼的 f 来自 kʷ(与 fünf 同理)。Viertel(四分之一、城区——Stadtviertel!)、Viereck(四边形)、unter vier Augen(私下——"四只眼睛之间")同族。|Vier (four) comes from Germanic *fedwōr, PIE *kʷetwóres — cognate with Latin quattuor (quadr-, quart-), Greek tettares (tetra-), Sanskrit catvā́ras and English four. The Germanic f comes from kʷ (as in fünf). Viertel (a quarter, and a city district — Stadtviertel!), Viereck (a quadrilateral) and unter vier Augen (in private — between four eyes) belong here.
d_fünf|fünf(五)来自日耳曼语 *fimf,印欧语 *pénkʷe——与拉丁语 quinque、希腊语 pente(Pentagon)、梵语 pañca(Punjab"五河")、英语 five 同根。Finger(手指)可能是它的派生:"五个一组的"。fünf gerade sein lassen(睁一只眼闭一只眼)、Fünfkampf、fünfte Kolonne(第五纵队)同族。|Fünf (five) comes from Germanic *fimf, PIE *pénkʷe — cognate with Latin quinque, Greek pente (Pentagon), Sanskrit pañca (the Punjab, five rivers) and English five. Finger may derive from it — one of five. Fünf gerade sein lassen (to turn a blind eye — to let five be even), Fünfkampf (a pentathlon) and die fünfte Kolonne (the fifth column) belong here.
d_sechs|sechs(六)来自日耳曼语 *sehs,印欧语 *swéḱs——与拉丁语 sex、希腊语 hex(Hexagon)、英语 six 同根。Sechseck(六边形)、Sechserpack、sechster Sinn(第六感)同族。|Sechs (six) comes from Germanic *sehs, PIE *swéḱs — cognate with Latin sex, Greek hex (hexagon) and English six. Sechseck (a hexagon), Sechserpack and der sechste Sinn (a sixth sense) belong here.
d_sieben|sieben(七)来自日耳曼语 *sebun,印欧语 *septḿ̥——与拉丁语 septem、希腊语 hepta、梵语 saptá、英语 seven 同根(甚至可能借给了闪米特语 šab'!)。Siebenschläfer(七眠子——6 月 27 日的天气谚语日,也指睡鼠!)、Siebenmeilenstiefel(七里靴)、sieben Sachen(全部家当)同族。注意:动词 sieben(过筛)是另一个词(Sieb 筛子)。|Sieben (seven) comes from Germanic *sebun, PIE *septḿ̥ — cognate with Latin septem, Greek hepta, Sanskrit saptá and English seven (and perhaps connected to Semitic šab'). Siebenschläfer (the Seven Sleepers' day, 27 June, a weather-lore day — and the dormouse!), Siebenmeilenstiefel (seven-league boots) and seine sieben Sachen (all one's belongings) belong here. The verb sieben (to sieve) is a different word (Sieb, a sieve).
d_acht8|acht(八)来自日耳曼语 *ahtau,印欧语 *h₁oḱtṓw——与拉丁语 octo、希腊语 oktō(Oktopus)、梵语 aṣṭā́、英语 eight 同根。它的形式像一个"双数",有人推测原义是"两个四"(两手不含拇指的四指),属猜想。注意:与 Acht(注意、achten)、Acht(放逐,in Acht und Bann)同形不同源!Achteck、Achterbahn(过山车——"8 字轨道"!)同族。|Acht (eight) comes from Germanic *ahtau, PIE *h₁oḱtṓw — cognate with Latin octo, Greek oktō (octopus), Sanskrit aṣṭā́ and English eight. Its form looks like a dual, and some guess it meant two fours (the four fingers of each hand without thumbs) — a conjecture. Beware the homographs Acht (attention, achten) and Acht (outlawry — in Acht und Bann): same shape, different words! Achteck (an octagon) and Achterbahn (a roller coaster — the figure-eight track!) belong here.
d_neun|neun(九)来自日耳曼语 *niwun,印欧语 *h₁néwn̥——与拉丁语 novem、希腊语 ennea、梵语 náva、英语 nine 同根。Neunauge(七鳃鳗——"九只眼",算上鼻孔和眼睛!)、neunmalklug(自作聪明)、die Neunziger 同族。|Neun (nine) comes from Germanic *niwun, PIE *h₁néwn̥ — cognate with Latin novem, Greek ennea, Sanskrit náva and English nine. Neunauge (a lamprey — nine eyes, counting gill openings!), neunmalklug (a smart-aleck — nine times clever) and die Neunziger (the nineties) belong here.
d_froh|froh(高兴的)来自日耳曼语 *frawaz(敏捷的、快活的),印欧语 *prew-(跳)存疑——与英语 frolic?(借自荷兰语 vrolijk)同源。Fronleichnam?不——那是 frô(主人)。fröhlich(快乐的)、Frohsinn(欢快)、frohlocken(欢呼)、Frohe Weihnachten!(圣诞快乐)、Frohbotschaft(福音——Evangelium 的德语)同族。|Froh (glad) comes from Germanic *frawaz (quick, merry), perhaps PIE *prew- (to leap); English frolic (from Dutch vrolijk) is cognate. Fröhlich (cheerful), Frohsinn (gaiety), frohlocken (to exult), Frohe Weihnachten! (Merry Christmas) and Frohbotschaft (the good news — the Gospel) belong here. Fronleichnam (Corpus Christi) is a different word, from frô, lord.
d_immer|immer(总是)是古高地德语 io mēr——io(曾经、总是,<*aiwi,与 ewig 同根!)+ mēr(更多)——"永远更多"。与 nimmer(不再——ni io mēr)、nimmermehr 是一对。immer wieder(一再)、für immer(永远)、immerhin(毕竟)、Immergrün(常春藤——"永远绿")同族。|Immer (always) is Old High German io mēr — io (ever, from *aiwi, the root of ewig!) + mēr (more): ever more. It pairs with nimmer (never again — ni io mēr) and nimmermehr. Immer wieder (again and again), für immer (forever), immerhin (after all) and Immergrün (periwinkle — evergreen) belong here.
d_nie|nie(从不)来自古高地德语 nio = ni(不)+ io(曾经,<*aiwi——与 ewig、immer 同根)——"没有一次"。英语 never(ne + ever)是完全同构的。niemals(从不)、niemand(没有人——ni + io + man!)、nirgends 同族。|Nie (never) comes from Old High German nio = ni (not) + io (ever, from *aiwi, the root of ewig and immer): not ever. English never (ne + ever) is built exactly the same way. Niemals (never), niemand (nobody — ni + io + man!) and nirgends (nowhere) belong here.
d_heut|heute(今天)来自古高地德语 hiu tagu("在这一天")——hiu 是指示代词 hie(这,与 hier 同根)的工具格,tagu 是 Tag。英语 today(to + day)同构。heutig(今天的)、heutzutage(如今)、Heute-Show、von heute auf morgen(一夜之间)同族。|Heute (today) comes from Old High German hiu tagu, on this day — hiu is the instrumental of the demonstrative hie (this, kin to hier), tagu is Tag. English today (to + day) is built the same way. Heutig (today's), heutzutage (nowadays) and von heute auf morgen (overnight) belong here.
d_gestern|gestern(昨天)来自日耳曼语 *gestra-,印欧语 *ǵʰyés——与拉丁语 heri(见 heri)、希腊语 chthes、英语 yesterday 同根。von gestern sein(过时了——"是昨天的")、vorgestern(前天)、das Gestern(往昔)同族。|Gestern (yesterday) comes from Germanic *gestra-, PIE *ǵʰyés — cognate with Latin heri (see heri), Greek chthes and English yesterday. Von gestern sein (to be old hat — to be from yesterday), vorgestern (the day before yesterday) and das Gestern (the past) belong here.
d_lied|Lied(歌)来自日耳曼语 *leuþą(歌、诗),来源不明,可能与拉丁语 laus(赞美)同根(存疑)。德国艺术歌曲 das Lied(舒伯特写了六百多首!)被英语法语原样借去(a lied, des lieder)。Volkslied(民歌)、Kinderlied、Nibelungenlied(尼伯龙根之歌)、Lieder ohne Worte(无词歌——门德尔松)同族。|Lied (a song) comes from Germanic *leuþą (a song, a poem), of unknown origin, possibly cognate with Latin laus (praise) — disputed. The German art song, das Lied (Schubert wrote over six hundred!), was borrowed as is into English and French. Volkslied (a folk song), Kinderlied, the Nibelungenlied and Mendelssohn's Lieder ohne Worte (Songs without Words) belong here.
d_trommel|Trommel(鼓)来自中古高地德语 trumbe/trumme(鼓、号),是拟声词——模仿"咚隆"声,与 Trompete(小号)同源。Trommelfell(鼓膜——"鼓皮"!)、die Werbetrommel rühren(大肆宣传——"敲招募鼓")、Die Blechtrommel(《铁皮鼓》,君特·格拉斯 1959)同族。|Trommel (a drum) comes from Middle High German trumbe, trumme (a drum, a trumpet), an imitative word for the rumbling boom, related to Trompete. Trommelfell (the eardrum — the drum-skin!), die Werbetrommel rühren (to beat the drum for something) and Günter Grass's Die Blechtrommel (The Tin Drum, 1959) belong here.
d_bühne|Bühne(舞台)来自中古高地德语 büne(高台、木板地板、阁楼),来源不明,可能与 Boden(地面)有关。它最初指任何抬高的木台,18 世纪才专指剧场舞台。über die Bühne gehen(进行、完成)、Bühnenbild(舞台布景)、Freilichtbühne(露天剧场)同族。|Bühne (a stage) comes from Middle High German büne (a raised platform, a plank floor, a loft), of unknown origin, perhaps linked to Boden (floor). It meant any raised boarding and only in the 18th century the theatre stage. Über die Bühne gehen (to take place, to go off), Bühnenbild (a stage set) and Freilichtbühne (an open-air theatre) belong here.
d_braut|Braut(新娘)来自日耳曼语 *brūdiz,来源不明——可能与 brauen(酿、煮)有关("做饭的人",存疑)。英语 bride 同源。Bräutigam(新郎)= Braut + gomo(男人,与拉丁 homo 同根!)——"新娘的男人";英语 bridegroom 的 groom 是后来附会进去的。Brautkleid(婚纱)、Brautpaar、Brautstrauß 同族。|Braut (a bride) comes from Germanic *brūdiz, of unknown origin — perhaps linked to brauen (to brew, to cook): the cook, disputed. English bride is cognate. Bräutigam (a bridegroom) is Braut + gomo (man, cognate with Latin homo!) — the bride's man; the groom of English bridegroom is a later reshaping. Brautkleid (a wedding dress), Brautpaar and Brautstrauß belong here.
d_kraut|Kraut(草、菜)来自日耳曼语 *krūdą,来源不明。它兼指"药草"(Heilkraut)与"白菜"(Sauerkraut 酸菜——英美士兵因此叫德国人 Krauts!)。Unkraut(杂草)、Kräutertee(草药茶)、Kräuterbutter、wie Kraut und Rüben(乱七八糟——"像菜和萝卜")同族。|Kraut (a herb, cabbage) comes from Germanic *krūdą, of unknown origin. It means both a herb (Heilkraut, a medicinal herb) and cabbage (Sauerkraut — which is why Allied soldiers called Germans Krauts!). Unkraut (weeds), Kräutertee (herbal tea), Kräuterbutter and wie Kraut und Rüben (higgledy-piggledy — like cabbage and turnips) belong here.
d_wiese|Wiese(草地)来自古高地德语 wisa,来源不明——可能与 Wasen(草皮)有关。慕尼黑啤酒节就在 Theresienwiese("特蕾莎草地",1810 年为王储婚礼命名)上举行,本地人简称 d'Wiesn。Blumenwiese、Wiesel(鼬——"草地上的"?存疑)同族。|Wiese (a meadow) comes from Old High German wisa, of unknown origin, perhaps linked to Wasen (turf). Munich's Oktoberfest is held on the Theresienwiese (Theresa's meadow, named for the crown prince's wedding in 1810), called die Wiesn by locals. Blumenwiese (a flower meadow) belongs here.
d_trän|Träne(眼泪)来自古高地德语 trahan(复数)的单数化,日耳曼语 *tahrą,印欧语 *dáḱru——与拉丁语 lacrima(古拉丁 dacrima!)、希腊语 dakry、英语 tear 同根:拉丁 lacrima 与德语 Träne 竟是同一个词。Tränengas(催泪瓦斯)、Freudentränen(喜极而泣)、Krokodilstränen(鳄鱼的眼泪)同族。|Träne (a tear) comes from a singular made from Old High German trahan, Germanic *tahrą, PIE *dáḱru — cognate with Latin lacrima (Old Latin dacrima!), Greek dakry and English tear: Latin lacrima and German Träne are one word. Tränengas (tear gas), Freudentränen (tears of joy) and Krokodilstränen (crocodile tears) belong here.
d_grab|Grab(坟墓)来自 graben(挖),日耳曼语 *grabaną,印欧语 *gʰrebʰ-(挖、刮)——与英语 grave、groove、engrave 同根:坟就是"挖出来的"。Graben(沟渠)、Grube(坑)、begraben(埋葬)、Begräbnis(葬礼)、Grabmal(墓碑)、sich im Grab umdrehen(在坟里翻身)同族。|Grab (a grave) comes from graben (to dig), Germanic *grabaną, PIE *gʰrebʰ- (to dig, to scratch) — cognate with English grave, groove and engrave: a grave is what is dug. Graben (a ditch), Grube (a pit), begraben (to bury), Begräbnis (a funeral), Grabmal (a tomb) and sich im Grab umdrehen (to turn in one's grave) belong here.
d_tal|Tal(山谷)来自日耳曼语 *dalą,印欧语 *dʰel-(凹陷)——与英语 dale、dell、古教会斯拉夫语 dolŭ(下面)同根。Neandertal(尼安德特河谷——尼安德特人 1856 年在此发现,Neander 是一位 17 世纪诗人的希腊化姓名"新人"!)、Talsperre(水坝)、Berg und Tal(山与谷)同族。|Tal (a valley) comes from Germanic *dalą, PIE *dʰel- (a hollow) — cognate with English dale and dell and Old Church Slavonic dolŭ (below). The Neandertal (where Neanderthal man was found in 1856 — Neander is the Greek form of a 17th-century poet's name, new man!), Talsperre (a dam) and über Berg und Tal (over hill and dale) belong here.
d_sumpf|Sumpf(沼泽)来自中古高地德语 sumpf,与 Schwamm(海绵)、英语 swamp、sump(集水坑)同根,可能出自印欧语 *swombʰ-(海绵状的)。versumpfen(变成沼泽,也指"泡在酒馆里")、Sumpfgebiet 同族。|Sumpf (a marsh) comes from Middle High German sumpf, related to Schwamm (a sponge), English swamp and sump, perhaps from PIE *swombʰ- (spongy). Versumpfen (to become boggy — and to get bogged down in a pub) and Sumpfgebiet (marshland) belong here.
d_nebel|Nebel(雾)来自日耳曼语 *nebulaz,印欧语 *nebʰ-(云、湿气)——与拉丁语 nebula(雾——同一个词!)、nubes?、希腊语 nephelē(云)、梵语 nábhas(天空)同根。Nebel 与拉丁 nebula 是教科书级的同源对。Nebelhorn(雾号)、nebelig(有雾的)、Nibelungen("雾之子"——尼伯龙根族名可能出自这里,存疑)同族。|Nebel (fog) comes from Germanic *nebulaz, PIE *nebʰ- (cloud, moisture) — cognate with Latin nebula (the same word!), Greek nephelē (a cloud) and Sanskrit nábhas (the sky). Nebel and nebula are a textbook cognate pair. Nebelhorn (a foghorn), neblig (foggy) and perhaps the Nibelungen (children of the mist — disputed) belong here.
d_frost|Frost(霜冻)来自 frieren(冻)的名词,日耳曼语 *frustaz,印欧语 *prews-(冻、烧)——与拉丁语 pruina(霜)、英语 freeze、frost 同根。Frostschutz(防冻)、Frostbeule(冻疮)、fröstelig(发冷的)、Bodenfrost 同族。|Frost comes from the noun of frieren (to freeze), Germanic *frustaz, PIE *prews- (to freeze, to burn) — cognate with Latin pruina (hoarfrost) and English freeze and frost. Frostschutz (frost protection), Frostbeule (a chilblain) and fröstelig (chilly) belong here.
d_strom|Strom(河流、电流)来自日耳曼语 *straumaz,印欧语 *srew-(流)——与希腊语 rheuma(流——Rheuma 风湿!)、rhythmos、梵语 sravati(流)、英语 stream 同根。1880 年代起 Strom 也指"电流":Stromrechnung(电费)、Strompreis、gegen den Strom schwimmen(逆流而上)、strömen 同族。|Strom (a river, a current) comes from Germanic *straumaz, PIE *srew- (to flow) — cognate with Greek rheuma (a flow — rheumatism!) and rhythmos, Sanskrit sravati and English stream. From the 1880s Strom also meant electric current: Stromrechnung (an electricity bill), Strompreis, gegen den Strom schwimmen (to swim against the tide) and strömen (to stream) belong here.
d_see|See 有两个性别两个意思:der See(湖)、die See(海)——都来自日耳曼语 *saiwiz(水域),来源不明。英语 sea 同源。Seele(灵魂)可能由此得名(见 Seele 条:"属于湖的")。Seemann(水手——"海的男人")、Seereise、Nordsee、Bodensee、Seehund(海豹——"海狗")、seekrank(晕船)同族。|See has two genders and two meanings: der See (a lake) and die See (the sea) — both from Germanic *saiwiz (a body of water), of unknown origin. English sea is cognate. Seele (the soul) may be named from it (see Seele: the one belonging to the lake). Seemann (a sailor — a sea man), Seereise, the Nordsee, the Bodensee, Seehund (a seal — sea dog) and seekrank (seasick) belong here.
d_hunger|Hunger(饥饿)来自日耳曼语 *hungruz,印欧语 *kenk-(烧、渴求)存疑——与英语 hunger、古挪威语 hungr 同根。Hungersnot(饥荒)、Heißhunger(食欲大开)、Hungerstreik(绝食)、Hungerlohn(饿不死的工资)同族。|Hunger comes from Germanic *hungruz, perhaps PIE *kenk- (to burn, to crave) — cognate with English hunger and Old Norse hungr. Hungersnot (a famine), Heißhunger (a ravenous appetite), Hungerstreik (a hunger strike) and Hungerlohn (starvation wages) belong here.
d_rad|Rad(轮)来自日耳曼语 *raþą,印欧语 *rot-h₂-(轮——"转动的")——与拉丁语 rota(轮——同一个词!)、梵语 ratha(战车)、立陶宛语 ratas 同根。轮子是印欧人的标志性发明,词根也跟着传遍各支。Fahrrad(自行车——"骑的轮")、Rad fahren、Riesenrad(摩天轮)、das fünfte Rad am Wagen(多余的人)同族。|Rad (a wheel) comes from Germanic *raþą, PIE *rot-h₂- (the turning thing) — cognate with Latin rota (the same word!), Sanskrit ratha (a chariot) and Lithuanian ratas. The wheel was a signature technology of the Indo-Europeans, and its name spread with it. Fahrrad (a bicycle), Rad fahren, Riesenrad (a Ferris wheel) and das fünfte Rad am Wagen (a fifth wheel — someone superfluous) belong here.
d_zorn|Zorn(愤怒)来自西日耳曼语 *turna-(撕裂、争吵),与荷兰语 toorn、古英语 torn(悲伤)同根,可能出自印欧语 *der-(撕)——与英语 tear(撕)同源:愤怒是"撕裂"。zornig(愤怒的)、Jähzorn(暴怒)、der Zorn Gottes(神怒)同族。|Zorn (anger) comes from West Germanic *turna- (tearing, strife), cognate with Dutch toorn and Old English torn (grief), probably from PIE *der- (to tear), like English tear: anger is a tearing. Zornig (angry), Jähzorn (a violent temper) and der Zorn Gottes (the wrath of God) belong here.
d_neid|Neid(嫉妒)来自日耳曼语 *nīþą(敌意、仇恨),与哥特语 neiþ、古英语 nīþ(恶意)同根,来源不明。Neidhammel(嫉妒鬼)、beneiden(羡慕)、neidisch、vor Neid platzen(嫉妒得要爆炸)、Neidkultur 同族。|Neid (envy) comes from Germanic *nīþą (hostility, spite), cognate with Gothic neiþ and Old English nīþ (malice), of unknown origin. Neidhammel (an envious person), beneiden (to envy), neidisch and vor Neid platzen (to burst with envy) belong here.
d_opfer|Opfer(祭品、牺牲者)来自 opfern(献祭),古高地德语 opfarōn,借自教会拉丁语 operari(做工→行礼拜)与 offerre(奉献)的混合——传教士把"做弥撒"译成它。今天 Opfer 兼"祭品"与"受害者"(Verkehrsopfer 交通事故遇难者、Opferrolle 受害者心态);青少年俚语 du Opfer!(你这个倒霉蛋)。|Opfer (a sacrifice, a victim) comes from opfern (to sacrifice), Old High German opfarōn, borrowed from a blend of Church Latin operari (to work, then to perform worship) and offerre (to offer) — missionaries used it for saying Mass. Today Opfer means both an offering and a victim (Verkehrsopfer a road casualty, Opferrolle a victim mentality); teenage slang du Opfer! means you loser.
d_süß|süß(甜)来自日耳曼语 *swōtuz,印欧语 *sweh₂dus——与拉丁语 suavis(见 suavis)、希腊语 hēdys、英语 sweet 同根。Süßigkeiten(甜食)、Süßstoff(代糖)、süß-sauer、Süße(甜美)、versüßen 同族。|Süß (sweet) comes from Germanic *swōtuz, PIE *sweh₂dus — cognate with Latin suavis (see suavis), Greek hēdys and English sweet. Süßigkeiten (sweets), Süßstoff (a sweetener), süß-sauer (sweet and sour) and versüßen (to sweeten) belong here.
d_bitter|bitter(苦)来自 beißen(咬)——"咬人的"味道:日耳曼语 *bitraz,印欧语 *bʰeyd-(劈、咬),与拉丁语 findere(劈——fissure)同根。英语 bitter、bite 同源。Bitterkeit(苦涩)、bitterböse(非常恶毒)、Magenbitter(苦味药酒)、Verbitterung 同族。|Bitter comes from beißen (to bite) — the taste that bites: Germanic *bitraz, PIE *bʰeyd- (to split, to bite), cognate with Latin findere (to split — fissure). English bitter and bite are cognate. Bitterkeit (bitterness), bitterböse (thoroughly nasty), Magenbitter (a digestif) and Verbitterung (embitterment) belong here.
d_sanft|sanft(温柔的)来自日耳曼语 *samftijaz(合适的、平和的),与 sammeln、zusammen 同根(*sem-"一起")——"合在一起的、协调的"。英语 soft 同源。Sanftmut(温和)、sanftmütig、besänftigen(安抚)同族。|Sanft (gentle) comes from Germanic *samftijaz (fitting, peaceable), kin to sammeln and zusammen (*sem-, together) — well fitted together, harmonious. English soft is cognate. Sanftmut (meekness), sanftmütig and besänftigen (to soothe) belong here.
d_ruder|Ruder(桨、舵)来自日耳曼语 *rōþrą,印欧语 *h₁reh₁-(划)——与拉丁语 remus(桨)、英语 rudder、row 同根。Ruder 在德语里兼"桨"与"舵":am Ruder sein(掌舵、执政)、aus dem Ruder laufen(失控)、rudern(划船)、Ruderboot 同族。|Ruder (an oar, a rudder) comes from Germanic *rōþrą, PIE *h₁reh₁- (to row) — cognate with Latin remus, English rudder and row. In German it means both oar and rudder: am Ruder sein (to be at the helm, in power), aus dem Ruder laufen (to get out of control), rudern (to row) and Ruderboot belong here.
d_segel|Segel(帆)来自日耳曼语 *seglą,来源不明——常有人联系到拉丁语 secare(切)——"切下的布",但并不可靠。英语 sail 同源。segeln(航行)、Segelflugzeug(滑翔机)、die Segel streichen(认输——"收帆")同族。|Segel (a sail) comes from Germanic *seglą, of unknown origin; it is often linked to Latin secare (to cut) — a cut piece of cloth — but unreliably. English sail is cognate. Segeln (to sail), Segelflugzeug (a glider) and die Segel streichen (to give up — to strike sail) belong here.
d_säul|Säule(柱子)来自日耳曼语 *sūl-(柱),与古英语 sȳl、哥特语 sauls 同根,来源不明。它是本族词,没有借拉丁的 columna(那给了 Kolumne"专栏")。Säulenhalle(柱廊)、Wirbelsäule(脊柱——"椎骨柱")、Litfaßsäule(广告柱——1855 年柏林印刷商 Litfaß 发明!)同族。|Säule (a column) comes from Germanic *sūl- (a post), cognate with Old English sȳl and Gothic sauls, of unknown origin. It is a native word — Latin columna gave German only Kolumne (a newspaper column). Säulenhalle (a colonnade), Wirbelsäule (the spine — the vertebral column) and Litfaßsäule (an advertising pillar, invented by the Berlin printer Litfaß in 1855!) belong here.
d_quell|Quelle(泉、来源)来自 quellen(涌出),日耳曼语 *kwel-,印欧语 *gʷelH-(涌)存疑。"泉"引申为"来源":Quellenangabe(注明出处)、Quellcode(源代码)、Informationsquelle、Einnahmequelle(收入来源)、Quellwasser 同族。|Quelle (a spring, a source) comes from quellen (to well up), Germanic *kwel-, perhaps PIE *gʷelH- (to gush). The spring became the source: Quellenangabe (a citation), Quellcode (source code), Informationsquelle, Einnahmequelle (a source of income) and Quellwasser (spring water) belong here.
d_silber|Silber(银)来自日耳曼语 *silubra,来源不明——很可能是借词,可能来自小亚细亚或闪米特语(阿卡德语 ṣarpu?)。它与斯拉夫语 serebro、波罗的语 sidabras 形近,说明是在欧洲北部流传的商贸词。拉丁语 argentum 是另一个词。Silberhochzeit(银婚)、Silberstreif am Horizont(一线希望)、silbern 同族。|Silber (silver) comes from Germanic *silubra, of unknown origin — probably a loan, perhaps from Asia Minor or Semitic (Akkadian ṣarpu?). Its likeness to Slavic serebro and Baltic sidabras suggests a trade word circulating in northern Europe. Latin argentum is a different word. Silberhochzeit (a silver wedding), Silberstreif am Horizont (a silver lining) and silbern belong here.
d_mahl|Mahl(餐)与 Mal(次、时刻)是同一个词:日耳曼语 *mēlą(规定的时间)——吃饭就是"固定的那个时间"。英语 meal 同源。Mahlzeit(一顿饭;也是德国办公室的午间问候!)、Abendmahl(晚餐、圣餐)、Festmahl(盛宴)同族。|Mahl (a meal) and Mal (a time) are the same word: Germanic *mēlą (an appointed time) — eating is the fixed time. English meal is cognate. Mahlzeit (a meal, and the German office's lunchtime greeting!), Abendmahl (supper, Holy Communion) and Festmahl (a banquet) belong here.
d_schwert|Schwert(剑)来自日耳曼语 *swerdą,来源不明,可能出自 *swer-(割、伤)。英语 sword 同源。Schwertlilie(鸢尾——剑形叶!)、Damoklesschwert(悬顶之剑)、Schwertfisch(剑鱼)同族。|Schwert (a sword) comes from Germanic *swerdą, of unknown origin, perhaps from *swer- (to cut, to wound). English sword is cognate. Schwertlilie (an iris — its leaves are sword-shaped!), Damoklesschwert (the sword of Damocles) and Schwertfisch (a swordfish) belong here.
d_truhe|Truhe(箱柜)来自古高地德语 truha,来源不明,可能与 Trog(槽)、英语 trough 同根——"挖空的木头"。Schatztruhe(宝箱)、Tiefkühltruhe(冷柜)、Truhenbank 同族。|Truhe (a chest) comes from Old High German truha, of unknown origin, perhaps kin to Trog (a trough) and English trough — a hollowed log. Schatztruhe (a treasure chest), Tiefkühltruhe (a chest freezer) and Truhenbank belong here.
d_fels|Fels(岩石)来自日耳曼语 *falisa,印欧语 *pel-(岩石)存疑——与古爱尔兰语 all(悬崖)、希腊语 pella(石头)比较。felsenfest(坚如磐石)、Felsen、Felswand(岩壁)、der Fels in der Brandung(浪中礁石——中流砥柱)同族。|Fels (a rock) comes from Germanic *falisa, perhaps PIE *pel- (rock), compared with Old Irish all (a cliff) and Greek pella (a stone). Felsenfest (rock-solid), Felsen, Felswand (a rock face) and der Fels in der Brandung (a rock in the surf — a tower of strength) belong here.
d_stier|Stier(公牛)来自日耳曼语 *steuraz,印欧语 *(s)táwros——与拉丁语 taurus(见 taurus)、希腊语 tauros、英语 steer 同根:德语 Stier 与拉丁 taurus 是同一个词。den Stier bei den Hörnern packen(抓住牛角——迎难而上)、Stierkampf(斗牛)、stieren(瞪眼)同族。|Stier (a bull) comes from Germanic *steuraz, PIE *(s)táwros — cognate with Latin taurus (see taurus), Greek tauros and English steer: German Stier and Latin taurus are one word. Den Stier bei den Hörnern packen (to take the bull by the horns), Stierkampf (a bullfight) and stieren (to stare) belong here.
d_eber|Eber(公猪)来自日耳曼语 *eburaz,印欧语 *h₁epero-——与拉丁语 aper(野猪——同一个词!)、古教会斯拉夫语 veprĭ 同根。约克 York 的古名 Eboracum 可能就是"野猪之地";人名 Eberhard("野猪般强壮")同族。|Eber (a boar) comes from Germanic *eburaz, PIE *h₁epero- — cognate with Latin aper (the same word!) and Old Church Slavonic veprĭ. York's Roman name Eboracum may be the place of boars, and the name Eberhard (strong as a boar) belongs here.
d_geheuer|Ungeheuer(怪物)是 un- + geheuer(熟悉的、安稳的)——"不熟悉、不安稳的东西";geheuer 来自古高地德语 gihiuri(亲切的),与 Heirat(婚姻)、heimisch 同根(*ḱey-"安居")。nicht ganz geheuer(有点可疑)、ungeheuer(巨大的——"大得吓人")、Ungeheuer von Loch Ness 同族。|Ungeheuer (a monster) is un- + geheuer (familiar, safe): the unfamiliar, unsafe thing. Geheuer comes from Old High German gihiuri (friendly), kin to Heirat (marriage) and heimisch (*ḱey-, to settle). Nicht ganz geheuer (a bit uncanny), ungeheuer (tremendous — frighteningly big) and das Ungeheuer von Loch Ness belong here.
d_speer|Speer(矛)来自日耳曼语 *sperą,与拉丁语 sparus(猎矛)同根,可能出自印欧语 *sper-(杆)。英语 spear 同源。Speerwurf(标枪)、Speerspitze(矛尖——先锋)同族。|Speer (a spear) comes from Germanic *sperą, cognate with Latin sparus (a hunting spear), perhaps from PIE *sper- (a pole). English spear is cognate. Speerwurf (the javelin) and Speerspitze (a spearhead, a vanguard) belong here.
d_schild|Schild(盾、招牌)来自日耳曼语 *skeldus(盾),印欧语 *(s)kel-(劈、切)——"劈开的木板"。英语 shield 同源。德语里 der Schild 是盾,das Schild 是招牌——同一个词按性别分义!Verkehrsschild(交通标志)、Namensschild、etwas im Schilde führen(心怀鬼胎——"盾上画着什么")、Schildkröte(乌龟——"带盾的蛤蟆"!)同族。|Schild (a shield, a sign) comes from Germanic *skeldus (a shield), PIE *(s)kel- (to split) — a split board. English shield is cognate. In German der Schild is a shield and das Schild is a sign — one word split by gender! Verkehrsschild (a road sign), Namensschild, etwas im Schilde führen (to be up to something — what's painted on one's shield) and Schildkröte (a tortoise — the shielded toad!) belong here.
d_waffe|Waffe(武器)来自日耳曼语 *wēpną,来源不明。英语 weapon 同源。Waffenstillstand(停火——"武器静止")、entwaffnen(缴械)、Waffenschein(持枪证)、die Waffen strecken(投降)同族。拉丁的 arma 另见 arm。|Waffe (a weapon) comes from Germanic *wēpną, of unknown origin. English weapon is cognate. Waffenstillstand (an armistice — weapons standing still), entwaffnen (to disarm), Waffenschein (a firearms licence) and die Waffen strecken (to lay down arms) belong here. Latin arma: see arm.
d_pfeil|Pfeil(箭)是罗马时代的早期拉丁借词:pilum(罗马军团的重标枪)——借入时 p 还没发生高地德语音变,后来才变成 pf。它与 Pfahl(桩,<palus)、Pflanze(<planta)、Pfund(<pondo)同一批。Pfeilspitze(箭头)、pfeilschnell(箭一般快)、Pfeiltaste(方向键)同族。|Pfeil (an arrow) is an early Latin loan of the Roman era: pilum, the legionary's heavy javelin — borrowed before the High German sound shift turned p into pf. It belongs to the same batch as Pfahl (a stake, from palus), Pflanze (from planta) and Pfund (from pondo). Pfeilspitze (an arrowhead), pfeilschnell (swift as an arrow) and Pfeiltaste (an arrow key) belong here.
d_tapfer|tapfer(勇敢的)来自日耳曼语 *dapraz(沉重的、坚定的),与荷兰语 dapper(勇敢)、英语 dapper(利落的)、古教会斯拉夫语 debelŭ(粗壮)同根:原义是"结实、有分量",后来才是"勇敢"。Tapferkeit(勇敢)同族。|Tapfer (brave) comes from Germanic *dapraz (heavy, firm), cognate with Dutch dapper (brave), English dapper and Old Church Slavonic debelŭ (thick): it first meant solid, weighty, and only later brave. Tapferkeit (bravery) belongs here.
d_ruhm|Ruhm(荣誉)来自古高地德语 hruom(名声、夸耀),与 rühmen(称颂)同根;人名 Rudolf("荣耀之狼")、Roland、Robert("光荣闪耀")里的 Ru-/Ro- 就是它。berühmt(著名的)、Ruhmesblatt(光荣的一页)同族。|Ruhm (fame, glory) comes from Old High German hruom (renown, boasting), kin to rühmen (to praise); the Ru- and Ro- of the names Rudolf (famed wolf), Roland and Robert (bright fame) are this word. Berühmt (famous) and Ruhmesblatt (a glorious page) belong here.
d_tugend|Tugend(美德)来自 taugen(有用、胜任)——"能干"就是美德:日耳曼语 *dugunþ,与英语 doughty(勇猛的)同根。拉丁的 virtus(男子气)走的是另一条路,但结果相同:美德原义都是"能力"。tugendhaft(有德的)、aus der Not eine Tugend machen(化不利为有利)、Tugend 与 Laster(恶习)同族。|Tugend (virtue) comes from taugen (to be fit, to be good for something) — ability is virtue: Germanic *dugunþ, cognate with English doughty. Latin virtus (manliness) took a different road to the same place: virtue first meant capability. Tugendhaft (virtuous), aus der Not eine Tugend machen (to make a virtue of necessity) and Tugend und Laster (virtue and vice) belong here.
d_saite|Saite(琴弦)来自日耳曼语 *saitiz(绳、套索),印欧语 *seh₂y-(绑)——与英语 sinew(筋)同根。andere Saiten aufziehen(换一种强硬态度——"换上别的弦")、Saiteninstrument(弦乐器)同族。注意:与 Seite(页、边)同音不同词!|Saite (a string) comes from Germanic *saitiz (a cord, a noose), PIE *seh₂y- (to bind) — cognate with English sinew. Andere Saiten aufziehen (to take a tougher line — to fit other strings) and Saiteninstrument (a stringed instrument) belong here. Not to be confused with Seite (a page, a side): same sound, different word!
d_asche|Asche(灰)来自日耳曼语 *askǭ,印欧语 *h₂eh₁s-(烧、干)——与拉丁语 ardere(见 ardor)、area 同根存疑;英语 ash 同源。Aschermittwoch(圣灰星期三)、Aschenputtel(灰姑娘——"灰堆里的小脏孩"!)、Aschenbecher(烟灰缸)、in Schutt und Asche(化为废墟)同族。|Asche (ash) comes from Germanic *askǭ, PIE *h₂eh₁s- (to burn, to dry) — perhaps kin to Latin ardere (see ardor) and area; English ash is cognate. Aschermittwoch (Ash Wednesday), Aschenputtel (Cinderella — the grubby little one in the ash heap!), Aschenbecher (an ashtray) and in Schutt und Asche (in ruins) belong here.
d_hohl|Höhle(洞穴)来自 hohl(空的),日耳曼语 *hulaz,印欧语 *ḱel-(遮盖、藏)——与 Hülle、Halle、Hölle、拉丁语 celare 同根:洞穴是"藏起来的空处"。英语 hollow、hole 同源。aushöhlen(掏空)、Höhlenmensch(穴居人)、sich in die Höhle des Löwen wagen(深入虎穴)同族。|Höhle (a cave) comes from hohl (hollow), Germanic *hulaz, PIE *ḱel- (to cover, to hide) — kin to Hülle, Halle, Hölle and Latin celare: a cave is a hidden hollow. English hollow and hole are cognate. Aushöhlen (to hollow out), Höhlenmensch (a caveman) and sich in die Höhle des Löwen wagen (to beard the lion in its den) belong here.
d_ufer|Ufer(河岸)是低地德语进入高地德语的词,日耳曼语 *ōbura-(斜坡、岸),与英语 over?(存疑)、古英语 ōfer(岸——英国地名 Over)同根。Flussufer、uferlos(无边无际的——"没有岸")、das rettende Ufer(安全的彼岸)同族。|Ufer (a riverbank) came into High German from Low German, Germanic *ōbura- (a slope, a shore), cognate with Old English ōfer (a bank — the English place name Over). Flussufer, uferlos (boundless — without shores) and das rettende Ufer (the saving shore) belong here.
d_teich|Teich(池塘)来自中古高地德语 tīch(池、堤),与 Deich(堤坝)是同一个词的两种发音——挖池塘的土堆成堤:英语 ditch(沟)与 dike(堤)也是这一对!Karpfenteich(鲤鱼塘)、der große Teich(大西洋——"大池塘",戏称)同族。|Teich (a pond) comes from Middle High German tīch (a pond, a dam), and Deich (a dyke) is the same word in another pronunciation — the earth dug from a pond was piled into a bank: English ditch and dike are the same pair! Karpfenteich (a carp pond) and der große Teich (the Atlantic — the big pond, jokingly) belong here.
d_grau|Grauen(恐怖)来自 grauen(使毛骨悚然),古高地德语 ingrūēn,与 grausam(残忍)、grässlich 同根——它与颜色 grau(灰)是两个不同的词!mir graut vor…(我害怕……)、grauenhaft、Morgengrauen(拂晓——这里的 grauen 是"变灰白",又是颜色词!)。|Grauen (horror) comes from grauen (to dread), Old High German ingrūēn, kin to grausam (cruel) and grässlich (hideous) — a different word from the colour grau (grey)! Mir graut vor… (I dread…), grauenhaft (horrible); and in Morgengrauen (daybreak) grauen is the colour word again, turning grey.
d_kahn|Kahn(小船)来自中古低地德语 kane,来源不明,可能与 Kanu?(不——那来自加勒比语)无关。Kahnfahrt(泛舟,施普雷森林的 Kahn 是特色!)、Lastkahn(驳船)同族;俚语 Kahn 也指"监狱"或"床"。|Kahn (a small boat) comes from Middle Low German kane, of unknown origin (unrelated to Kanu, which is Caribbean). Kahnfahrt (a punt trip — the Kähne of the Spreewald are famous!) and Lastkahn (a barge) belong here; in slang Kahn can also mean jail or bed.
d_bell|bellen(狗吠)是拟声词,日耳曼语 *bellaną,与英语 bell(钟——"响的东西")、bellow(吼)同根。Hunde, die bellen, beißen nicht(会叫的狗不咬人)、Gebell 同族。|Bellen (to bark) is imitative, Germanic *bellaną, kin to English bell (the ringing thing) and bellow. Hunde, die bellen, beißen nicht (barking dogs don't bite) and Gebell (barking) belong here.
d_kehl|Kehle(喉咙)来自日耳曼语 *kelǭ,印欧语 *gʷelH-(吞)——与拉丁语 gula(见 gul)同根。aus voller Kehle(放声)、in die falsche Kehle bekommen(误解而生气——"吞进错的喉咙")、Kehlkopf(喉头)同族。|Kehle (the throat) comes from Germanic *kelǭ, PIE *gʷelH- (to swallow) — cognate with Latin gula (see gul). Aus voller Kehle (at the top of one's voice), etwas in die falsche Kehle bekommen (to take something the wrong way — down the wrong throat) and Kehlkopf (the larynx) belong here.
d_klau|Klaue(爪)来自日耳曼语 *klawō,与英语 claw、clew(线团——古义"球")同根,印欧语 *gleu-(成团、抓握)存疑。动物的爪子用来抓——于是口语里 klauen(偷,"用爪子抓走"!)也来自它;Klaue 在俚语里还指"潦草的字"(eine furchtbare Klaue 字写得像爪子扒的)。Vogelklaue(鸟爪)、in den Klauen des…(落入某人魔爪)同族。|Klaue (a claw) comes from Germanic *klawō, cognate with English claw and clew. Klauen (to nick, to steal — to claw!) and Klaue (scrawl, slang) belong here.
d_schwanz|Schwanz(尾巴)来自中古高地德语 swanz(拖曳、摆动),与 schwanken(摇晃)、schwingen 同根——"摆动的东西"。den Schwanz einziehen(夹着尾巴——认怂)、kein Schwanz(一个人也没有,俚)同族。|Schwanz (a tail) comes from Middle High German swanz (a trailing, swaying), kin to schwanken (to sway) and schwingen — the swinging thing. Den Schwanz einziehen (to tuck one's tail in, to back down) belongs here.
d_fell|Fell(兽皮)来自日耳曼语 *fellą,印欧语 *pel-(皮)——与拉丁语 pellis(见 pellis)、英语 fell、film 同根:德语 Fell 与拉丁 pellis 是同一个词。ein dickes Fell haben(脸皮厚)、jemandem das Fell über die Ohren ziehen(骗人——"剥皮盖过耳朵")、seine Felle davonschwimmen sehen(眼看希望落空)同族。|Fell (a pelt) comes from Germanic *fellą, PIE *pel- (skin) — cognate with Latin pellis (see pellis), English fell and film: German Fell and Latin pellis are one word. Ein dickes Fell haben (to be thick-skinned), jemandem das Fell über die Ohren ziehen (to fleece someone) and seine Felle davonschwimmen sehen (to see one's hopes float away) belong here.
d_ries|Riese(巨人)来自古高地德语 riso,来源不明,可能与 Recke?(否)无关;有人比作古挪威语 risi。Riesenrad(摩天轮)、riesig(巨大的)、Riesengebirge(巨人山脉)——在德语里 Riesen- 是最常用的"超大"前缀(Riesenhunger、Riesenspaß)。|Riese (a giant) comes from Old High German riso, of unknown origin; Old Norse risi is compared. Riesenrad (a Ferris wheel), riesig (huge) and the Riesengebirge (the Giant Mountains) — in German Riesen- is the favourite prefix for enormous (Riesenhunger, Riesenspaß).
d_gespenst|Gespenst(幽灵)来自古高地德语 gispensti(诱惑、魔影),出自 spanan(诱惑、引诱)——"引人上当的幻象"。马克思与恩格斯《共产党宣言》(1848)开篇:Ein Gespenst geht um in Europa(一个幽灵在欧洲游荡)。gespenstisch(阴森的)、Hausgespenst 同族。|Gespenst (a ghost) comes from Old High German gispensti (an enticement, a phantom), from spanan (to entice) — the deluding apparition. Marx and Engels open the Communist Manifesto (1848) with Ein Gespenst geht um in Europa — a spectre is haunting Europe. Gespenstisch (eerie) belongs here.
d_meng|Menge(人群、数量)来自古高地德语 managī(多),与 manch(一些)、英语 many 同根——"许多"。数学的 Menge 是"集合"(Mengenlehre 集合论——康托尔!)、jede Menge(大量)、Menschenmenge(人群)同族。|Menge (a crowd, a quantity) comes from Old High German managī (a multitude), kin to manch (many a) and English many. In mathematics a Menge is a set (Mengenlehre, set theory — Cantor!); jede Menge (loads) and Menschenmenge (a crowd) belong here.
d_strudel|Strudel(漩涡)来自中古高地德语 strudel,与 strodeln(沸腾)同根,可能出自印欧语 *srew-(流,与 Strom 同根)。奥地利的 Apfelstrudel 因卷起的面皮像漩涡而得名!|Strudel (a whirlpool) comes from Middle High German strudel, kin to strodeln (to bubble), perhaps from PIE *srew- (to flow, like Strom). The Austrian Apfelstrudel is named for its swirl of rolled pastry!
d_wirbel|Wirbel(漩涡、椎骨)来自 werben(转动)的古义,日耳曼语 *hwirb-(转),与英语 whirl 同根。Wirbelsäule(脊柱)、Wirbelsturm(旋风)、Wirbel machen(大肆张扬)、Trommelwirbel(鼓点)同族。|Wirbel (a whirl, a vertebra) comes from Germanic *hwirb- (to turn), cognate with English whirl. Wirbelsäule (the spine), Wirbelsturm (a cyclone), Wirbel machen (to make a fuss) and Trommelwirbel (a drum roll) belong here.
d_tropf|Tropfen(滴)来自 triefen(滴落),日耳曼语 *drupô,与英语 drop、drip 同根。ein Tropfen auf den heißen Stein(杯水车薪——"热石上的一滴")、tropfen、Tropf(点滴、也指可怜虫)同族。|Tropfen (a drop) comes from triefen (to drip), Germanic *drupô, cognate with English drop and drip. Ein Tropfen auf den heißen Stein (a drop in the ocean — a drop on a hot stone), tropfen (to drip) and Tropf (a drip, and a poor wretch) belong here.
d_dampf|Dampf(蒸汽)来自日耳曼语 *dampaz,与英语 damp(潮湿)同根。19 世纪工业革命让它成了时代关键词:Dampfmaschine(蒸汽机)、Dampfer(汽船)、Dampflok、Dampf ablassen(发泄——"放汽")、dampfen 同族。|Dampf (steam) comes from Germanic *dampaz, cognate with English damp. The Industrial Revolution made it a keyword: Dampfmaschine (a steam engine), Dampfer (a steamer), Dampflok, Dampf ablassen (to let off steam) and dampfen belong here.
d_schwefel|Schwefel(硫)来自日耳曼语 *swebla-,来源不明,可能与拉丁 sulphur 有远亲(存疑)。Schwefelsäure(硫酸)、Schwefelholz(火柴——旧称!)、Pech und Schwefel(形影不离——"沥青与硫磺")同族。|Schwefel (sulfur) comes from Germanic *swebla-, of unknown origin, perhaps distantly related to Latin sulphur (disputed). Schwefelsäure (sulfuric acid), Schwefelholz (a match — the old word!) and wie Pech und Schwefel (inseparable — like pitch and brimstone) belong here.
d_becher|Becher(杯)是早期借词:中世纪拉丁 bicarium(杯),出自希腊语 bikos(陶罐)。英语 beaker 同源。Joghurtbecher(酸奶杯)、Zahnputzbecher、bechern(豪饮,俚)同族。|Becher (a beaker) is an early loan: Medieval Latin bicarium (a cup), from Greek bikos (an earthen jar). English beaker is cognate. Joghurtbecher (a yoghurt pot), Zahnputzbecher and bechern (to booze, slang) belong here.
d_schlamm|Schlamm(泥浆)来自中古高地德语 slam,与英语 slime?(存疑)、低地德语 slamm 同根。Schlammschlacht(泥潭战——比喻:互相抹黑)、verschlammt、Schlammlawine 同族。|Schlamm (mud, sludge) comes from Middle High German slam, cognate with Low German slamm and perhaps English slime. Schlammschlacht (a mudfight — mudslinging), verschlammt (silted up) and Schlammlawine (a mudslide) belong here.
d_fessel|Fessel(镣铐)来自 fassen(抓)——"绑住的东西":日耳曼语 *feteraz,与英语 fetter、拉丁语 pes(脚——绑脚的!)同根存疑。fesseln(捆绑、吸引——ein fesselndes Buch 引人入胜的书!)、entfesseln(释放)同族。|Fessel (a fetter) is linked to fassen (to grasp) — the thing that binds: Germanic *feteraz, cognate with English fetter and perhaps Latin pes (the foot — a foot-binding!). Fesseln (to bind, and to captivate — ein fesselndes Buch, a gripping book!) and entfesseln (to unleash) belong here.
d_folter|Folter(酷刑)来自中世纪拉丁 poledrus(小马驹——一种形似木马的刑架),经古高地德语 foltar。jemanden auf die Folter spannen(吊人胃口——"把人绑上刑架")、foltern、Folterkammer 同族。|Folter (torture) comes from Medieval Latin poledrus (a foal — a rack shaped like a wooden horse), through Old High German foltar. Jemanden auf die Folter spannen (to keep someone in suspense — to stretch them on the rack), foltern and Folterkammer belong here.
d_erbarm|Erbarmen(怜悯)来自 arm(贫穷)——er- + barmen(<be-armen"为穷人动情"):路德时代把拉丁 misereri(怜悯,<miser 可怜)译成"对可怜人动心"。Barmherzigkeit(慈悲——misericordia 的德语直译:"可怜+心"!)、erbärmlich(可怜的、糟糕的)同族。|Erbarmen (pity) comes from arm (poor) — er- + barmen (from be-armen, to feel for the poor): German rendered Latin misereri (to pity, from miser, wretched) as being moved by the wretched. Barmherzigkeit (mercy — a direct German rendering of misericordia: poor + heart!) and erbärmlich (pitiful, dreadful) belong here.
d_mild|mild(温和的)来自日耳曼语 *milduz,印欧语 *mel-(软)——与拉丁语 mollis(见 mollis)、英语 mild、melt 同根。Milde(宽和)、mildern(减轻,mildernde Umstände 减刑情节)、Mildtätigkeit(慈善)同族。|Mild (gentle) comes from Germanic *milduz, PIE *mel- (soft) — cognate with Latin mollis (see mollis) and English mild and melt. Milde (clemency), mildern (to mitigate — mildernde Umstände, mitigating circumstances) and Mildtätigkeit (charity) belong here.
d_wild|wild(野的)来自日耳曼语 *wilþiz,来源不明。英语 wild 同源。Wild(野味)、Wildnis(荒野)、Wildschwein(野猪)、wildfremd(完全陌生的)、der Wilde Westen 同族。|Wild comes from Germanic *wilþiz, of unknown origin; English wild is cognate. Wild (game), Wildnis (wilderness), Wildschwein (a wild boar), wildfremd (a total stranger) and der Wilde Westen belong here.
d_staun|staunen(惊奇)是 18 世纪从瑞士德语进入标准语的词,原义"发呆、愣住"(stūnen),与英语 stun?(存疑,多认为来自古法语)无关。erstaunlich(惊人的)、Erstaunen、bestaunen 同族。|Staunen (to marvel) entered standard German from Swiss German in the 18th century, first meaning to stare, to be dazed (stūnen); English stun is probably unrelated (usually traced to Old French). Erstaunlich (astonishing), Erstaunen and bestaunen belong here.
d_blick|Blick(目光)来自 blicken(看),原义"闪光"——日耳曼语 *blik-,与 blinken(闪烁)、bleich(苍白)、英语 bleak 同根:目光是"眼里的一道闪光"。Augenblick(瞬间——"眨眼间")、Anblick(景象)、Überblick(概览)、Ausblick(展望)同族。|Blick (a glance) comes from blicken (to look), originally to flash — Germanic *blik-, kin to blinken (to twinkle), bleich (pale) and English bleak: a look is a flash of the eye. Augenblick (a moment — the blink of an eye), Anblick (a sight), Überblick (an overview) and Ausblick (a prospect) belong here.
d_flieh|fliehen(逃)来自日耳曼语 *þleuhaną,与英语 flee 同根;名词 Flucht(逃亡)。它与 fliegen(飞)不同源,但两者在德语里互相影响。Flüchtling(难民)、Fluchtweg(逃生通道)、flüchtig(匆匆的;在逃的)、Zuflucht(避难所)同族。|Fliehen (to flee) comes from Germanic *þleuhaną, cognate with English flee; its noun is Flucht (flight). It is not the same root as fliegen (to fly), though the two have influenced each other. Flüchtling (a refugee), Fluchtweg (an escape route), flüchtig (fleeting, on the run) and Zuflucht (refuge) belong here.
d_eil|Eile(匆忙)来自 eilen,古高地德语 īlen,来源不明。Eilzug(快车)、eilig(急的)、Eilbrief(快信)、Eile mit Weile(欲速则不达——festina lente 的德语!)同族。|Eile (haste) comes from eilen, Old High German īlen, of unknown origin. Eilzug (a fast train), eilig (urgent), Eilbrief (an express letter) and Eile mit Weile (more haste, less speed — German's festina lente!) belong here.
d_irr|Irrtum(错误)来自 irren(迷路、犯错),日耳曼语 *irzijaną,印欧语 *h₁ers-(游荡)——与拉丁语 errare(游荡、犯错——error!)同根:德语 irren 与拉丁 errare 是同一个词,都把"走错路"理解成"弄错"。Irrgarten(迷宫)、Irrlicht(鬼火)、irre(疯的;超棒的!)同族。|Irrtum (an error) comes from irren (to stray, to err), Germanic *irzijaną, PIE *h₁ers- (to wander) — cognate with Latin errare (to wander, to err — error!): German irren and Latin errare are one word, both taking a wrong turning as a mistake. Irrgarten (a maze), Irrlicht (a will-o'-the-wisp) and irre (crazy — or brilliant!) belong here.
d_ewig|ewig(永恒的)来自古高地德语 ēwīg,出自 ēwa(永恒、法律)——日耳曼语 *aiwaz,印欧语 *h₂eyu-(生命力、年岁),与拉丁语 aevum(见 aeternitas)、希腊语 aiōn、英语 ever 同根。Ewigkeit(永恒)、das ewige Leben、Ewigkeitsklausel(永恒条款——《基本法》第 79 条第 3 款,不可修改的宪法核心!)同族。|Ewig (eternal) comes from Old High German ēwīg, from ēwa (eternity, law) — Germanic *aiwaz, PIE *h₂eyu- (vital force, age), cognate with Latin aevum (see aeternitas), Greek aiōn and English ever. Ewigkeit (eternity), das ewige Leben and the Ewigkeitsklausel (the eternity clause, Article 79(3) of the Basic Law, the unamendable core of the constitution!) belong here.
d_bach|Bach(小溪)来自日耳曼语 *bakiz,与英语 beck(北英格兰方言"溪")、荷兰语 beek 同根,来源不明。大量德语地名与人名都是它:Bach 家族("溪边的人")、Offenbach、Wiesbaden?(否,那是 Bad)、Bachstelze(鹡鸰——"溪边高跷")。den Bach runtergehen(完蛋——"顺溪而下")同族。|Bach (a brook) comes from Germanic *bakiz, cognate with English beck (a northern dialect word) and Dutch beek, of unknown origin. Countless German names contain it: the Bach family (people by the brook), Offenbach, Bachstelze (a wagtail — brook stilt). Den Bach runtergehen (to go down the drain) belongs here.
d_trauer|Trauer(哀悼)来自 trauern,古高地德语 trūrēn(垂下眼睛、低头),来源不明——与 treu(忠诚)、trauen(信任)无关,只是字形相近。Trauerfeier(追悼会)、Trauerspiel(悲剧——Tragödie 的德语)、Trauerweide(垂柳——"哀悼的柳")、trauern um 同族。|Trauer (mourning) comes from trauern, Old High German trūrēn (to cast down one's eyes), of unknown origin — unrelated to treu (faithful) or trauen (to trust), despite the look. Trauerfeier (a memorial service), Trauerspiel (a tragedy — German's own word), Trauerweide (a weeping willow) and trauern um (to mourn for) belong here.
d_schreit|Schritt(步)来自 schreiten(跨步),日耳曼语 *skrīdaną,与古英语 scrīþan(走动)同根。Fortschritt(进步——"向前的一步")、Schrittmacher(起搏器——"定步伐者")、Schritt für Schritt、überschreiten(越过)、einschreiten(干预)同族。|Schritt (a step) comes from schreiten (to stride), Germanic *skrīdaną, cognate with Old English scrīþan (to move about). Fortschritt (progress — a step forward), Schrittmacher (a pacemaker — the pace-setter), Schritt für Schritt (step by step), überschreiten (to exceed) and einschreiten (to intervene) belong here.
`;
  RAW.trim().split("\n").forEach(line => {
    const i = line.indexOf("|"), j = line.lastIndexOf("|");
    if (i < 0 || i === j) return;
    ROOT_STORIES.de[line.slice(0, i)] = [line.slice(i + 1, j), line.slice(j + 1)];
  });
})();
